WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:06.779
Do you remember being 16, maybe 17 years old,
you're living with your mommy and your daddy

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:12.190
and every time you wanted to do something,
your mom and dad would say, "We pay the rent

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.190
here.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:14.190
You do what we tell you to do."

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.619
Well, when you turn 18 and get your own apartment
complex, you live in your own place, privacy,

00:00:18.619 --> 00:00:20.880
all this stuff, it feels good, doesn't it?

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.189
The same goes with you as an entrepreneur
when you finally go and sign your own lease

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:29.449
and you feel free because this is your business,
this is your place of operation, you get to

00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:31.710
do what you want to do with your business.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:38.300
Now, prior to doing that, my goal today with
this video is to make sure you are as equipped

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.370
and educated about signing a lease, because
I think after watching this there's not really

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:46.109
a book you can read bout leases and all that,
because it would never sell that many copies.

00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:51.719
But in this video, my goal is for you to know
exactly A-Z what you need to know as a tenant

00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:55.420
with the brokers motivation, the landlord's
motivation, everyone's motivation so when

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.149
you go into it, you're educated, you don't
get ripped off, you get the best deal, and

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:00.739
get the best possible thing for yourself.

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:04.199
So today's video is directed to three different
types of people.

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:06.259
One, you don't yet know if you're going to
have an office.

00:01:06.259 --> 00:01:09.450
You're an entrepreneur, you just want to get
educated about office leases.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.450
Great.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.490
Two, you're about to sign your first office
lease and you don't know what you're doing.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:18.149
Or three, you already have your own office
lease, but you want to know if you did it

00:01:18.149 --> 00:01:20.840
right or wrong so the next time you don't
make the same mistake.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.810
So, I want you to know that everything I talk
about in this video is from many mistakes

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.939
I've made, and many leases I've signed.

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:32.689
I've signed millions and millions and millions
of dollars of leases over my career and I

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:34.649
started off small and built it to what it
is today.

00:01:34.649 --> 00:01:35.649
So let's get right into it.

00:01:35.649 --> 00:01:40.790
A few things you've got to keep in mind as
you're about to sign an office lease.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.630
First things first, let's solve for X.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.549
One, how much space do you need?

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:49.510
Realistically, how much space do you need
and when you think about how much space do

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:54.500
you need, not how much space do you need today,
because if you're going to sign a 36-month

00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:00.700
lease, it's almost asking yourself how much
space do you need 12-18 months from now.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:04.369
Because you need a little bit of room for
you to outgrow your office, but you don't

00:02:04.369 --> 00:02:05.689
want it to be way too big.

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:07.409
You don't want it to be way too small.

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:09.700
You need to plan for 12-18 months.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:10.700
Number two, location.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.170
Where do you want it to be?

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:13.750
Does it have to be near a freeway?

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.360
Does it matter if it's near a freeway?

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.950
Do you want it to be in a place where all
the traffic comes?

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.730
Do you want it to be in a shopping center
where it's right by the mall?

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:21.730
Do you want it to be in a mall?

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:22.730
Where do you want it to be?

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:25.090
Where's it suitable for you as a location.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.090
Three, affordability.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.090
What can you afford?

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.269
What is the number that you can afford right
now and the way to calculate this is how much

00:02:31.269 --> 00:02:32.680
money do you have set aside.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:38.530
So, if your expenses are going to be $4,000
a month, and you take your personal expenses,

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:42.220
whatever it's going to be, do you have that
saved up for six to 12 months?

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.730
Because they're going to ask you for first
month, last month.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.480
Sometimes they ask you for first, second and
last month.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.200
It depends on if you have experience, how
you negotiate.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.680
Now keep this part in mind.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.230
Anything when it comes down to commercial
real estate, it's all negotiable, all of it.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.300
There's so many creative ways to do it.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:02.280
No two deals in commercial real estate are
ever the same.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.430
Number four, term.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:07.959
Twelve months, 24 months, 36 months, what
is your term?

00:03:07.959 --> 00:03:09.150
Five is traffic.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.780
Do you need parking?

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.620
So meaning is there going to be a lot of employees
that are going to be parking their car?

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.209
Do you want it to be the type of parking where
it's $1.20 per hour that people come in and

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:19.209
the gate goes up?

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:20.209
Do you need security?

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:21.540
What is it that you're looking for with parking?

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.931
Foot traffic meaning is it a place that a
lot of customers are going to come in and

00:03:24.931 --> 00:03:27.439
if it is, then you need a nice office space.

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:30.951
If it's not, who cares if they're not going
to see it because it's a call center, there's

00:03:30.951 --> 00:03:35.099
not really going to be any customers that
are going to come and buy from you.

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:38.551
So you need to know that part because it's
a big difference on price point that you get

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:39.551
to save.

00:03:39.551 --> 00:03:40.551
Then last but not least, legal.

00:03:40.551 --> 00:03:43.780
You always want to, whatever you do, whatever
deal you get, even if your commercial real

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.470
estate agent, your broker says, "I'm going
to have my legal look at it," I always recommend

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:53.910
you spend $200 - $400 to another attorney
that's not emotionally attached to him [broker]

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:57.549
because to them, their attorney's going to
look at the deal that benefits you, but still

00:03:57.549 --> 00:04:00.480
at the same time it's the deal that's happening
here with the broker.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.280
Get a non-interested attorney to look at the
entire deal before you do anything.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.019
So a couple of basic things about office space.

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:09.550
You have basically three different types of
office space you're going to get if you're

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:11.780
running a commercial office space.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.819
Type A, type B, type C. You keep hearing about
this.

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:16.500
People ask, what is type A, type B, type C?

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.320
Type A is newest building.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:26.320
It was built in 2009, in the most traffic
place community is type A.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.250
Type B may be it's a little older but it's
in a decent community.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:33.280
So it was built in 1981, but it's in a decent
community.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.140
It could be type B. Still presentable, still
clients can come in, customers can come in,,

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:38.750
but it's not the sexiest.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:44.830
Then there's type C. Type C is oldest, worst
location, not a desirable place is type C.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:47.760
By the way, I've had a type C, type B and
a type A office.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.280
I know all of them.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.520
And I know exactly what the benefits, pros,
cons, pricing wise that it is to go in each

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:53.520
one of these things.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.520
So you need to know about them.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.890
Now, let's talk about rent.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:05:00.300
A lot of times you'll hear this language about
single net, double net, triple net, full-service

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.300
gross.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.080
Pat, what does it really mean when the commercial
real estate agent starts talking about this?

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.080
I'm kind of confused.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.920
So a couple of things to keep in mind.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.160
You rarely will see single net being done.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:11.940
This is when the landlord pays the entire
rent.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.840
This is when the landlord pays the entire
rent, entire expenses.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.500
So, for instance, if you have taxes, insurance
and maintenance, they're covering all that.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:22.550
You're not paying any of that.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:23.550
It's all on them.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.250
They're saying, "We're willing to take single
net."

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:26.650
You're not going to find that a lot.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:29.840
Maybe in another country you live in, you're
not going to find that a lot in America.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.520
Double net is where you're only paying taxes
and insurance.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.780
Triple net is you're paying taxes, insurance,
and maintenance.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:42.930
Then obviously full service gross, you're
paying one check, it covers everything, full-service

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:43.930
gross.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:45.150
It kind of works out that way as well.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.570
I've done all of them myself.

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:49.130
Typically commercial's going to be double
or triple net.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.110
That is very common to be hearing that.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:55.790
So now, credit wise, my first office lease
I signed, I'll never forget this.

00:05:55.790 --> 00:06:00.230
I was in an office that I was renting from
other people, and this place was horrible

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:01.780
for me because I couldn't grow.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:02.780
I had no control.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:04.710
It was driving me insane.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.780
And I had no authority to say anything about
the office that I had.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:09.340
So I want to do this, no, it's not your office.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:10.340
I want to do that, no.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:13.050
Kind of like the whole thing of living with
mommy and daddy, which they have all the right

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:14.050
to do that.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.490
So one day I made a decision.

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:16.490
I'm saving money.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:17.710
I saved my money.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:21.970
One night I'm talking to one of my agents,
a guy named Oscar.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:23.710
If he sees this he'll remember this.

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:26.150
I said, "Oscar, I want you to put me in front
of somebody."

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:27.880
He said, "I don't think this is for me.

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:30.330
I'm having a hard time with sales and this,
this that."

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:33.300
He wanted to go become an agent for sports
athletes, which he eventually did.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.790
He became an agent for famous soccer players.

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:38.550
He said, "I'm just not good in sales.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:39.550
And I don't know if I can."

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:42.140
I said, "Introduce me to one person before
you say this isn't for you."

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:44.420
So that night we get in his Volkswagen.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:45.420
We go to Granada Hills.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:51.490
I go to this Type C office building and we
go into this place and the guy we meet is

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:52.490
a guy named Shane.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:55.060
And I walked in and the entire place is dark.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:59.050
This is '07, '08, the mortgage crisis is already
taking place.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:00.210
This guy was killing it.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:01.920
He no longer is.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:02.920
And I go to his office.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:06.700
We end up doing all his investments, savings,
insurance, all this other stuff.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.580
And at the same time, I say, "What's going
on with this office?"

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:10.220
He said, "Man, I can't do anything with this
office."

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:11.510
I said, "What's your plans with this office?"

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:13.870
He said, "I'm trying to lease this out to
somebody."

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:16.570
I knew my credit was bad credit.

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:19.580
So I knew a building's not going to give me
a direct lease.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.410
So I had bad credit, I need a sublease.

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:27.190
We started talking, he and I. I remember vividly
what price point I got, what I paid for it.

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:28.630
How it worked out.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:30.110
So I got the lease away from him.

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:31.110
I paid for the rent.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:32.170
I paid for the electric.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:33.170
I paid everything.

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:36.030
He ended up getting good deal because I didn't
know what I was doing at that time with leases.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:39.990
But I ended up getting a good deal because
I didn't have a whole five-year lease that

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:40.990
I signed.

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.670
I got a shorter sign and it worked out for
me because I didn't have good credit.

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:49.090
So it allowed me a way to build credit with
a building and start moving myself up in the

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.380
business and all this other stuff.

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:51.470
So that worked out for me.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:55.700
I stayed there, grew incredibly well until
eventually we got evicted out of that office

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:59.660
because we grew extremely too fast and then
I had to go get my next office space.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:02.500
Let me get into the next point I want to make
to you here.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:07.450
So motivation, motivation, this is very important
for you to know.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:09.360
What is your motivation?

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.130
What is the broker's motivation?

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.870
What is the building owner's motivation?

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:18.300
Extremely important you understand this part
and what relationship you play.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.300
I coach entrepreneurs all the time about this
here.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:23.330
Okay, so hear me out.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:27.060
First of all, what is your motivation?

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:33.610
Your motivation is to get exactly the amount
of space you're looking for, with exactly

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:38.010
the floor plan, you know, the way you want
it to be done, which means TI - you'll hear

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:39.010
this terminology.

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:41.950
TI, that means tenant improvement.

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:46.120
You want them to set up the office in the
way that you want it to be set up, without

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.070
extending the lease too far.

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:49.070
Okay.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:54.190
So you want it to be three years, with TI,
with office set up the way it is, with the

00:08:54.190 --> 00:09:00.550
lowest amount of expenses you're paying, and
that's your motivation as a person that's

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:01.550
getting an office space.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:03.450
Now let me tell you the broker.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.260
Brokers go with you, some of the brokers are
going to watch this.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:08.520
We had, who were these brokers that showed
up the other day?

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.410
Real nice guys, four brokers came, they're
Valuetainment followers.

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:13.760
They were in our building and showed up from
out of nowhere.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:15.770
Four classy guys.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:18.590
And I'm talking brokers can be watching this
right now and I highly recommend you guys

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:21.610
share this with other commercial real estate
agents.

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:24.920
Brokers commission works this way.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:31.260
The longer the lease, the bigger the space,
the higher dollar per square feet, the bigger

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:32.260
their payday.

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.210
Let me simplify the math for you.

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:38.380
Let's just say the space you get is $3,000
a month.

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:40.520
And you sign a 36-month lease.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:44.530
$3000 x 36 is $108,000.

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:47.750
The commission typically is 6%.

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:51.820
Six percent on the $108,000 is $6,480.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:56.900
Let me tell you how this $6,480 gets split
and who pays for it.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:01.200
Half of it goes to your real estate agent,
that you don't pay for.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.170
The building owner pays for it.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:08.070
The other half goes to the broker that represents
the building owner.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:12.840
So when your broker gets half of this [$6,480],
and the building owner's broker gets the other

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:13.840
half.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:17.970
So $3,200 goes to your broker, $3,200 gets
to the building owner's broker.

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:18.970
You don't pay for it.

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:20.080
They [building owner] pay for it.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.190
Now, if you're on the complete opposite side,
and you're subleasing your space to somebody

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:31.920
else, you are paying this $6,480 to the two
because now you're becoming the lease owner,

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:32.920
not them.

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:34.180
Keep that part in mind.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:36.010
Now, let's talk about the broker relationship.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:42.320
I've had many different brokers in my career
and they're pretty interesting.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:45.840
One personality I had, let's call this guy
Hal Cook.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:51.580
I would call this guy probably the sleaziest
commercial real estate agent I've ever dealt

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:52.640
with in my life.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:54.810
And so let me tell you what happened with
this guy.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:11:00.530
He came in and I told at the time when we
signed this lease, I told him, I signed this

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:04.740
lease that was a six-year lease, I told him
I only need it for three years because I'm

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:06.060
moving to Dallas, Texas.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.250
He knew it.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:13.410
So he came and he pushed and said, why don't
you take this part for three years, and take

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:18.120
the other part for six years but what we're
going to do is we're going to put a cancellation

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:23.980
on the 36-month so we can make this work and
cancel it and then you can go to Texas, because

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:25.480
that's what's going to happen.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:26.800
No problem.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:28.680
So it came to we signed everything.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:32.750
The contract ended up being a million and
a half or two million dollars so do the math

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:34.050
here on six percent.

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:35.050
Okay?

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:39.270
He made I think $84,000 or some number like
that that was paid out.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:40.270
He made a lot of money.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:44.790
The guy that was working the lease at that
time with him later on came back and said,

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:46.430
everybody hates working with this guy.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:49.220
That guy even left him because of the way
he handled the whole thing.

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:50.220
Now watch what happened.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.350
Experience with this broker.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:55.500
He did it with his own commercial real estate
company.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:12:00.560
So he called himself John Doe's, Hal Cook's
realty.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.080
Let's just say hypothetically that's the name
of the company.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:04.080
Okay?

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:05.460
So he called this company that name.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:07.240
He was the only employee.

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:12.170
When it came down to the three years that
I had to leave to Texas, he wouldn't answer

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:16.820
my phones, he wouldn't call me, and he told
me he had a heart attack.

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:19.980
So then I went to the landlord and I said,
wait a minute, this guy told me he had a heart

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:22.192
attack and another guy named Tony told me,
what are you talking about?

00:12:22.192 --> 00:12:23.192
He never had a heart attack.

00:12:23.192 --> 00:12:25.450
He was just showing a property this week.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:28.690
So then he says, I can't help you because
my wife told me not to help you.

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:29.700
This guy disappears.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:31.620
Obviously, a crook.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:36.070
And the whole story why I'm telling you this
in business is you're going to face guys like

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:37.070
this.

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:38.300
And I call them the way it is.

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:39.300
I'll never forget.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:42.860
I call them the way it is with how this guy
handled the entire deal.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:44.640
This is the point I'm making to you.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.710
My suggestion, don't get a broker that is
not tied to a bigger company.

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:53.930
I would much rather hire somebody that's tied
to CB Richard Ellis that's a very big company

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:58.920
and know that if my real estate agent ends
up quitting the industry, I can always go

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:02.230
to CB Richard Ellis and say, "I need your
help for your attorneys to look at this and

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.120
help bail me out of this."

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:08.090
Because this guy was a nobody, and a one-man
shop, I got stuck with this lease.

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:11.850
So I ended up paying it, the whole million
and a half, you know, two million dollar lease,

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:12.850
whatever it was.

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:14.630
We're good to go because we're going to hold
our commitment.

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:15.630
But that's the broker side.

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:16.742
Now let me give you another guy.

00:13:16.742 --> 00:13:20.060
Another guy name I had, a guy named Jared.

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:22.160
Absolute stud.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:23.430
He made one mistake.

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:27.550
He took one lease of mine that he said he
knows the Glendale market very well.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:28.550
He didn't.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:32.170
So you need to know whoever your broker becomes,
what city they're experience is.

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:33.170
Listen.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:34.170
Brokers are salespeople.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:35.830
They'll tell you they know everything about
every market.

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:36.830
They do not.

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:37.920
Everybody has a specialty.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.700
So ask them what is your specialty?

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:42.090
What market, what city, what zip code.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.240
Let them tell you.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:45.211
If they tell you everywhere, don't believe
it.

00:13:45.211 --> 00:13:46.320
It's a lie.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:49.850
Ask them, give me specific zip codes that
you work very well.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:55.310
So find commercial real estate agents that
are dominate in the specific zip code that

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:59.779
you are trying to get an office space or lease
your office space to somebody else.

00:13:59.779 --> 00:14:00.839
So I like Jared.

00:14:00.839 --> 00:14:01.839
He was a hustler.

00:14:01.839 --> 00:14:02.839
He worked hard.

00:14:02.839 --> 00:14:03.839
He answered.

00:14:03.839 --> 00:14:04.839
He was all good.

00:14:04.839 --> 00:14:05.839
He just took one bad lease.

00:14:05.839 --> 00:14:06.839
I gave him a lot of leases.

00:14:06.839 --> 00:14:09.640
I think he's done four, five, six, seven,
eight, ten leases with us.

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:10.640
Good guy.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:13.240
Now, let me give you another relationship
about brokers you've got to know about.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:19.120
Just so you know, the building owner's broker
and your broker.

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:21.900
I know this is going to sound strange and
the commercial real estate agents are going

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:26.710
to think that I'm crazy when I'm saying this,
they're on the same damn team.

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:28.020
They're on the same team.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.470
Do you know why?

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:31.230
What do you think they both want to happen?

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:32.310
To close the deal.

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:35.810
And how big of a lease do you think both of
them want to happen?

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:41.661
As big of a lease as possible, square footage
wise, as high of a number as possible, as

00:14:41.661 --> 00:14:45.360
long of a term as possible makes both of them
more commission.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:50.230
So the only people that are on the same team
are the building owner's broker and your broker.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:52.910
I know this is very weird, but that's exactly
how it is.

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:56.620
Even if that broker tells you that's not right,
that's exactly how it is.

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:57.620
Okay?

00:14:57.620 --> 00:14:58.980
So the way they talk to each other, they go
like this.

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:01.930
They go and say, hey, what do you think this
is a bad.

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:04.480
. . all this stuff that they use in negotiation
language.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:05.600
You know him where's he at?

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:06.800
Does he like this space?

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:08.830
Obviously he likes this space, so what are
we going to do?

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:12.080
They go talk, they strike up a deal, then
you sign the lease.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.060
Let me tell you what I got them to do.

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:15.110
And then they come and talk to you.

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:16.110
Okay?

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:18.480
Just know these two brokers are on the same
team.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:19.810
You've got to understand that part.

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:24.720
Nothing wrong with that, but don't be naive
and thinking your own broker is fully on your

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:25.720
side.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:27.370
They want a longer lease to make more money
on it.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:29.340
So now let's go into the building owner.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:33.380
The building owner's trying to keep this property
because they're building equity.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:36.930
Every year somebody else is paying the rent
for them to the building they own.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.520
They're building more equity, long term, they're
building a bigger portfolio.

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:43.120
The more income comes in, the more properties
they can leverage, the more assets they can

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:44.120
leverage.

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:45.580
They're simply running the capitalistic system.

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:47.110
They're doing their part.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:53.920
They have no involvement except for leasing,
making sure 100% of the office space is being

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:54.920
rented out.

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:59.210
So an office space that's above 90% they don't
need you that bad.

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:02.750
An office space that's less than 70%, they
need you tremendously.

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:06.790
So it's always good to ask what percentage
of this office is occupied already?

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:08.890
They'll say, we're around 70%.

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:10.540
It's on your side.

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:13.339
Oh, we're at 92%, it's on their side.

00:16:13.339 --> 00:16:16.010
So if you really want to be in that building,
you're going to pay premium.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:17.480
Know that position.

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:18.480
Occupancy.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:21.800
What is the occupancy rate in the office right
now, in this building right now?

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:22.800
They'll tell you.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.380
So I've worked directly with building owners.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:28.520
I've worked with bigger building owners where
I only dealt with the broker.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.630
But regardless, you kind of want to know what's
going on.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:37.899
Now another thing when you're dealing with
building owners you're leasing out of, sometimes

00:16:37.899 --> 00:16:42.370
if the company you're working with that owns
and manages the building is public, they're

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:43.370
cutthroat.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:45.150
They are cutthroat.

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:47.029
Douglas Emmett, cutthroat.

00:16:47.029 --> 00:16:48.620
There is no wiggle room.

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:51.940
You don't matter as customer service as much
because to them it's shareholders.

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:52.940
Cutthroat.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:57.050
So especially when they need to do a lease,
right before the quarter, they're not letting

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.111
you go because it goes on the loss statement,
and they don't want to show that as hey, we

00:17:00.111 --> 00:17:01.649
had to let this lease go.

00:17:01.649 --> 00:17:05.470
There is no listen, I'm going through challenging
times with my family, I want you to work with

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:06.470
me.

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:07.470
Zero.

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:08.470
If they're public.

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:12.300
If they're private, they'll typically understand
you a little bit more, so in this scenario,

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:18.550
I'd prefer going into building spaces that
is privately held, not publicly held.

00:17:18.550 --> 00:17:22.670
And you'll understand that as you research
this a little bit more and you go from experience

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:24.550
you'll get a better idea what I mean by this
part here.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:25.550
So I covered this, I covered that.

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:26.550
I covered the broker side, relationship side.

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.360
Okay, let me tell you what recently happened
with an office space I'm dealing with, on

00:17:28.360 --> 00:17:30.860
how creative these things can be.

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.820
So, we currently have outgrown this office
space.

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:36.950
It's pretty obvious, everyone's climbing on
top of each other, we can't fit in this place

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:37.950
anymore.

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:41.740
And when we got this lease, 15 months ago,
14 months ago, is that about the timeline?

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:45.220
Fourteen months ago, when we moved here from
Dallas, we needed an office space.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:46.620
CB Richard Ellis came and helped us out.

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:48.230
They did a great job.

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:50.640
Classy people on what they did.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:54.250
We like them, at least the people that were
working with us, we liked them.

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:56.110
And it all worked out very well.

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:57.110
Beautiful building.

00:17:57.110 --> 00:18:01.800
I think this place is 98%, it's one of the
most desirable buildings in Dallas.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:02.850
Morgan Stanley's in here.

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:07.890
It's all the hedge fund managers, the money
managers and type A. It's right off the freeway,

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.160
great location, Galleria.

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:12.430
It's exactly where you want your office space
to be.

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:14.700
But we've outgrown it.

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:18.720
And if I wanted to take an office space within
here, it has to be completely on the opposite

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:19.720
side.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:20.720
It's pretty technical.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:24.330
So we found out, we went and looked at a couple
of buildings to buy, and some of the buildings

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:28.780
we wanted to buy right now it's kind of over
market price because Texas real estate is

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:31.330
doing well, so everything is a little bit
higher.

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:35.700
And no one's really selling anything right
now because everybody's keeping it because

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:36.840
all these companies are moving in.

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:40.960
So you're paying 30% more than you would typically
pay to buy a building.

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:42.550
So we said, no, we're not going to buy a building
right now.

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:47.240
We were almost close, we almost made an offer
to this one building, 40,000 square foot building

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:49.460
but we said no, we'll pass on this.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:53.130
So all of a sudden, we found out about this
other office space.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:54.640
It's a massive office space.

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:59.559
It's pretty much an entire floor, somewhere
around 20,000 square feet.

00:18:59.559 --> 00:19:04.990
And we go in and their motivation and our
motivation is a different motivation.

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:09.540
They're motivation is they moved to Arizona.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:12.380
They don't need as much space here anymore.

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:15.200
And they just need a smaller office space.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:16.510
We need a bigger office space.

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:19.020
So we went in and we struck up a deal.

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:23.500
The entire place is beautiful place, incredibly
taken care of.

00:19:23.500 --> 00:19:28.920
Training facility, offices, kitchen, it's
just a ridiculous place, how it's set up.

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:30.460
You'll see it here when we do it.

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:34.530
So the way what happened is there was a commercial
real estate agent representing me, a broker

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:36.810
representing me that I hadn't yet signed a
commitment with them.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:38.370
I met with them.

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:40.210
He didn't know a lot about commercial real
estate.

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:42.440
Sweetheart, great man, Christian guy.

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:44.000
But he didn't know a lot about commercial
real estate.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:45.100
I was asking him questions.

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:46.410
I said, "How long have you been doing this
for?"

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:49.900
So he came from a different real estate side
and he's trying to do commercial real estate.

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:52.850
And he sent me an email saying, "I don't think
you need us" so this time we didn't use a

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:53.850
broker.

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:58.850
So the whole money's being made by the building
owner's broker.

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:06.560
Long story short, long story short, the tenant
currently that wants to lease me the space,

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:13.700
his broker that he and I spoke, I just didn't
like this guy, the way that he approached

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:14.700
us.

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:16.190
I said, look, I can't deal with you.

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:17.610
And I said, we're not doing the deal.

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:19.110
I have no interest in doing the deal.

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:21.080
he started giving me the whole thing.

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:22.160
Everybody wants this office space.

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:24.540
It's the best thing in town and all this other
things.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:26.510
I said, "You're overpriced!"

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:28.140
You have no idea what you're talking about.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:31.770
I said, "Educate your client!

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:33.440
Educate your client!

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:36.930
You are doing a disservice for not educating
your client.

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:39.360
Tell them what's going on in the marketplace.

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:41.710
What is the sublease rate right now in the
marketplace?

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:43.580
What are you doing right now with this office
space?

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:44.580
Tell them!"

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:46.600
No, you don't know, we're going to get this
thing leased in the next couple of weeks.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:47.600
It's a beautiful building.

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:48.600
I said, "Really?

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:52.150
Then why is it you've had it on the market
for six months and this person's losing forty-some

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:55.020
thousand dollars a month and you have it in
the marketplace, whatever the number is, you

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:56.020
have it in the marketplace."

00:20:56.020 --> 00:20:57.429
No, we're going to keep it.

00:20:57.429 --> 00:20:58.429
I said, "great."

00:20:58.429 --> 00:20:59.600
I set him aside.

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:01.850
So one day I'm going home, I said, you know
what?

00:21:01.850 --> 00:21:04.730
I'm going to go meet with the CEO directly.

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:06.010
That's what I'm going to do today.

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:07.010
So I talked to Tom.

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:08.560
I said, guys, I'm going to go meet with the
CEO.

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:10.660
And I said, let me just call him first before
I go visit him.

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:12.190
I texted him and said, "hey," I found out
his number.

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:13.429
I said, "do you mind if you and I talk?

00:21:13.429 --> 00:21:15.060
I called his home office, 800 number.

00:21:15.060 --> 00:21:16.720
I finally got to the CEO.

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:18.740
And I said, I want to speak with the CEO.

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:20.280
Very nice guy.

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:22.560
And I said, "Hey, this is Patrick, Patrick
Bet-David.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.580
I want to rent out your space.

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:26.710
Do you mind if you and I deal together?

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:29.190
I don't want to speak to your commercial real
estate agent.

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:32.600
Although you signed a contract, which means
you have to pay him the commission.

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:35.460
But I think you and I need to deal direct,
because I don't have a real estate agent representing

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:36.460
me right now.

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:37.460
Are you okay with that?"

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:38.460
Yes.

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:39.630
I went through the whole scenario.

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:41.679
Everything I did the math for him.

00:21:41.679 --> 00:21:45.350
This is what I need, this is what I'm willing
to pay, if you don't do this, I already have

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:48.770
another place I can go to that I can build
it out exactly how I want.

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:50.650
I went through every single thing with me.

00:21:50.650 --> 00:21:52.340
This is my situation.

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:53.570
This is where I'm at.

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:55.290
This is what I'm offering you.

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:59.000
I want you to spend the entire weekend thinking
about it, but here's one caveat.

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:00.020
He said, "What's that?"

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:02.450
I don't want you to call your commercial real
estate agent.

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:03.580
He said, "What do you want me to do?"

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:08.770
I said, I want you to call other commercial
real estate agents in the marketplace and

00:22:08.770 --> 00:22:13.549
I want you to act as if you're me, trying
to lease your space.

00:22:13.549 --> 00:22:17.670
Give them all the details that you know about
your office space.

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:22.679
And you ask that commercial real estate agent
that's not emotionally involved or money-wise

00:22:22.679 --> 00:22:23.920
involved, ask them.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:26.930
Is it a fair deal that this guy is giving
me?

